4,Music of Madagascar — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Music_of_Madagascar,"The music of Madagascar comprises the diverse traditional and contemporary styles performed by the Malagasy people, reflecting the island's settlement by Austronesian migrants from Southeast Asia around the 5th to 10th centuries CE and subsequent Bantu arrivals from East Africa, which produced a distinctive synthesis of melodic chordophones and rhythmic percussion. [1][2] Traditional Malagasy ...","Malagasy, Tesaka",Austronesian
5,Traditional music in Madagascar,https://www.musicinafrica.net/magazine/traditional-music-madagascar/,"Traditional music in Madagascar formed its identity with influences from various horizons: the Austronesian peoples on the one hand, and African, Arab and European migratory populations on the other. Such variety made it impossible to specify, in time and space, the true origin of traditional music in Madagascar. What is certain is that traditional music was probably heard for the first time ...","Malagasy, Tesaka",Austronesian
6,Malagasy Folk Music - Melodigging,https://www.melodigging.com/genre/malagasy-folk-music,"Malagasy folk music is the traditional music of Madagascar, shaped by the island's Austronesian roots and centuries of contact with mainland Africa and the Indian Ocean world.","Malagasy, Tesaka",Austronesian
7,A deep dive into Malagasy traditional music - vivytravel.com,https://www.vivytravel.com/a-deep-dive-into-malagasy-traditional-music/,"Explore the richness of traditional Malagasy music, its instruments, vocal styles, and cultural significance across Madagascar's regions.","Malagasy, Tesaka",Austronesian
8,"Madagascar: History, Culture, and Geography of Music",https://sk.sagepub.com/ency/edvol/the-sage-international-encyclopedia-of-music-and-culture/chpt/madagascar-history-culture-geography-music,"Madagascar, an island in the Indian Ocean, a crossroads of trade between Africa and Asia, a land of intrinsic cultural richness, is known for its diversity and specificity. Successive migrations to the Big Island over many centuries have provided an exceptional cultural, historical, and linguistic heritage. Traces of successive layers of Austronesian, East Indies, Arabic, Bantu, East African ...","Malagasy, Tesaka",Austronesian
